@database RetroWP Help

@node Main "RetroWP Help"
Welcome to RetroWP, the portable word processor.

RetroWP is edited with a vi-style modal editor: most keys are
commands, not typed text, until you press "i" to start typing.
With a mouse, a click puts the caret where you clicked and a
Shift+click selects from the caret to there.

Pick a topic:

{Normal Mode|Normal}
{Insert Mode|Insert}
{Visual Mode|Visual}
{Command Line|CommandLine}
{Undo and Redo|UndoRedo}
{Format Menu and Shortcuts|Format}
{Printing|Print}
{About RetroWP|About}
@endnode

@node Normal "Normal Mode"
Normal mode is where RetroWP starts, and where Escape always
returns you to. Keys move the cursor or act on text; they are
never inserted as typed characters.

Movement:
  h  l        left / right one character
  j  k        down / up one paragraph
  w  b  e     forward / back a word, to word end
  W  B  E     the same, but by WORD (blanks only, punctuation
              counts as part of the word)
  0  ^  $     start of line, first non-blank, end of line
  gg  G       start / end of the document
  {  }        back / forward a paragraph block
  3w          a number before a motion repeats it (any motion)

Editing:
  x           delete the character under the cursor
  d + motion  delete from cursor to where the motion lands
  dd          delete the whole current paragraph
  c + motion  like d, then enters Insert mode
  cc          change the whole current paragraph
  y + motion  yank (copy) into the register
  yy          yank the whole current paragraph
  p  P        put the register after / before the cursor
  u           undo
  Ctrl+R      redo

Entering Insert mode:
  i  a        insert before / after the cursor
  I  A        insert at the first non-blank / end of the line
  o  O        open a new paragraph below / above, and insert

{Back to Main|Main}
@endnode

@node Insert "Insert Mode"
Insert mode is where typed characters actually go into the
document. It behaves like an ordinary text editor:

  any printable key   inserts that character
  Enter               splits the paragraph in two
  Backspace           deletes the character before the cursor,
                       merging into the previous paragraph at
                       the start of a line
  Escape               returns to Normal mode

Everything typed during one Insert session -- from entering
Insert mode until Escape -- undoes as a single step with "u",
not one keystroke at a time.

The current Bold / Italic / Underline / Size toggles (see the
Format topic) apply to whatever you type next, the same way
they did before RetroWP had a modal editor at all.

{Back to Main|Main}
@endnode

@node Visual "Visual Mode"
Visual mode selects a range of text to operate on.

  v            start a charwise selection
  V            start a linewise selection (whole paragraphs)
  any motion   extends the selection
  d  x         delete the selection
  y            yank the selection
  c            change the selection (delete, then Insert mode)
  Ctrl+X Ctrl+C  cut or copy the selection to the system clipboard
  Ctrl+V       paste over the selection (Edit menu does the same)
  Escape        cancel, back to Normal mode

{Back to Main|Main}
@endnode

@node CommandLine "Command Line"
Press ":" to open the command line, or "/" to search forward.

Ex commands:
  :w      save (to the file the document came from, or ask for a name)
  :w name save under that name, which the document then keeps
          (.rtf writes RTF, .md Markdown, .odt and .pdf go through
          the gateway; anything else is plain text)
  :e name open that file (.rtf is read as RTF, .md as Markdown,
          .odt through the gateway, WordStar by content)
  :q      quit
  :wq     save and quit
  :x      save and quit (same as :wq)

Anything else after ":" is not recognized and just beeps.

Search:
  /text<Enter>   jump to the next occurrence of "text",
                  wrapping around the end of the document
  n              repeat the last search forward

{Back to Main|Main}
@endnode

@node UndoRedo "Undo and Redo"
  u        undo the last change
  Ctrl+R   redo

A whole Insert-mode session, or a compound command like "cw",
undoes as one step -- you do not need to press "u" once per
keystroke to get back to where you started.

{Back to Main|Main}
@endnode

@node Format "Format Menu and Shortcuts"
Bold, Italic, and Underline toggle what typed text looks like
from here on -- they do not need a selection.

  Ctrl+B   toggle Bold
  Ctrl+I   toggle Italic
  Ctrl+U   toggle Underline

Font Size and Alignment open a picker dialog listing the
available choices.

  Ctrl+]   choose a font size
  Ctrl+L   choose left / center / right alignment

The Format menu also lists Bold, Italic, Underline, Size, and
Alignment directly.

The ruler bar across the top of the window does the same with
the mouse. Its buttons, left to right:

  New, Open, Save
  Cut, Copy, Paste, Undo
  Font, Size, Bold, Italic, Underline
  Align Left, Center, Align Right, Justify
  Single, 1.5 and Double line spacing
  Indent Less, Indent More (half an inch at a time)
  Page Break (the text after the caret starts a new page; at
              the start of a paragraph it turns that paragraph's
              break on or off)
  Section Break (the text after the caret starts a new section:
              a new page with its own paper, margins, header and
              footer, which start as this section's. Backspace
              at the start of a section removes the break)
  Header, Footer (a line printed at the top or bottom of every
              page; the Format menu has them too on the Amiga
              and Mac)
  Page Setup (the paper -- Letter, A4, Legal or A5, upright or
              on its side -- or the margins; the File menu has
              it too on the Amiga and Mac)

Font lists the typefaces this machine has: the GDOS fonts on
the ST, the fonts in FONTS: on the Amiga, the installed fonts on
the Mac (DOS has one). Default is the machine's own. With text
selected, Font, Size and Bold change that text; with nothing
selected they change what you type next, until you move the
caret. Typing anywhere else takes the style of the text around
it. A font this machine does not have is shown in one like it
-- a serif for Times, a typewriter face for Courier -- and kept
in the file under its own name.

Page Setup's Margins asks in the status line, in inches: one
length for all four margins, or four for left, right, top and
bottom ("1 1 0.75 0.75"). A number may say cm or mm instead.
Paper and margins belong to the section the caret is in.

Header and Footer ask in the status line. Type the line and
press Enter (Esc leaves it as it was). A | splits it into parts:
one part is centred, two go left and right, three left, centre
and right. A # is the page number. For example:

  My Report|Page #

A button shown pressed in (inverted on a black-and-white
screen) is the one in force at the caret. The ruler under the
buttons measures the page's text column in inches: the
triangles are the caret paragraph's first-line, left and right
indents, and an L marks a tab stop.

Drag a triangle to move that indent; it snaps to an eighth of
an inch. The top one is the first line alone; the bottom-left
one is the rest of the paragraph, and the first line stays
where it was, so dragging it right makes a hanging indent.
Drag a tab stop to move it, or off the ruler to remove it.
Click the ruler to set a tab stop there; click a stop to
remove it. Paragraph buttons, indents and tab stops act on
every paragraph the selection touches, or the caret's.

On DOS the buttons are letters in brackets and the ruler is a
row of dots: [ and ] are the margins, > the first-line indent,
| the left indent, < the right, and L R C D the tab stops.
Where > and | share a cell, dragging there moves both.

{Back to Main|Main}
@endnode

@node Print "Printing"
File > Print (Ctrl+P; on DOS also Shift+F7, as in WordPerfect)
prints the document, on one of two printers:

  The gateway's -- a printer the offload gateway (the Pi on the
  serial line) has. It prints the document as its PDF would
  look: the fonts, sizes and styles, pictures left out.

  This machine's -- the printer port: PRN: on the ST, PRT: on
  the Amiga (through the Printer preferences), PRN on DOS. It
  prints at ten characters to the inch and six lines to the
  inch, with the page's paper and margins, the paragraphs'
  alignment, indents, tab stops and spacing, and the header
  and footer. On the ST and DOS you choose what the printer
  understands -- Plain text, Epson, HP PCL or PostScript --
  and, for PCL and PostScript, two-sided printing; RetroWP
  remembers it. Epson, PCL, PostScript and the Amiga print
  bold, italic and underlined text; fonts are not printed.

When both are there you are asked which; the gateway's is
picked first. The Mac and gem4xe print on the gateway's only.

On the gateway's printer you then see what it offers: Copies,
Two-sided (long or short edge), Colour or Gray, Paper feed and
Collate, as the printer has them. Pick one to change it, then
Print.

{Back to Main|Main}
@endnode

@node About "About RetroWP"
RetroWP is an open-source, portable word processor for vintage
platforms. Its core editing engine is written in strict,
portable C, with zero platform-specific calls anywhere in the
engine itself -- the same engine runs unchanged on an Atari ST,
inside this help viewer, and in this project's own automated
test suite on a modern PC.

Heavier features -- large-dictionary spell checking, a
thesaurus, grammar checking, and document format conversion --
are available through an optional offload link to a small
companion device, so machines with as little as 1MB of RAM stay
fast and responsive without needing that data loaded locally.

{Back to Main|Main}
@endnode
